   - 7 of 7 unknown kebab names differ (ui_tree writes `foo-bar`; Reagent writes `fooBar`, which the DOM holds as `foobar`);
   - 86 of 216 all-lowercase spellings differ (ui_tree canonicalises them; Reagent passes them through).
   ui_tree's SECOND half, `dom-attr-aliases` (ui_tree.cljc:409, public), IS the right table to reuse.

Where the dossier and Codex AGREED: the direction (option A); the conversion sits at the two body walkers and not inside `attr-string`; head and shell bags stay verbatim; the refusals judge the converted name; no new API or warning system.

Where they DIFFERED, and what decided each point:
(a) STRING KEYS. The dossier said "a string key stays verbatim". Codex said a string key skips Reagent's step but NOT React's alias step. Codex is right, by source:
    - reagent-slim's `dash-to-prop-name` (template.cljs:366-381) and stock Reagent 2.0.1's `util/dash-to-prop-name` (read from the local reagent-2.0.1.jar) both return a string key unchanged, so it reaches React as the prop name;
    - react-dom then aliases it (19.3.0 `aliases` Map, e.g. `["htmlFor","for"]`, plus `pushAttribute` special cases).
    So {"className" "card"} must write `class`, while {"hx-post" "/x"} stays `hx-post` because it has no alias.
(b) FALSE CLASS MEMBERS. The dossier said "nils dropped"; Codex said nil AND false. Both adapters' `class-names` keep a member only when it is truthy (slim template.cljs:674-701 `(when c …)`; stock util.cljs `(if c …)`), so false drops too. Codex is right.
(c) CUSTOM ELEMENTS. The dossier left this uncertain; Codex supplied the answer, now verified:
    - stock Reagent 2.0.1 routes a tag containing "-" (`HiccupTag` `custom`) through a SEPARATE `custom-prop-name-cache` with no class/for/charset seeds;
    - slim uses its ordinary cache for every string tag (template.cljs:765, `dom-element? (string? (.-tag parsed))`);
    - react-dom 19.3.0 server, for a tag containing "-" (react-dom-server.node.development.js, the `-1 !== type.indexOf("-")` branch about :3151-3207), aliases ONLY `className` → `class` and writes every other prop name verbatim (e.g. `htmlFor`, `strokeWidth`).
