## Ruling

When the target frame's drain is on the current stack, `destroy-frame!` records a **pending destroy** and returns. The current event then runs to completion as an ordinary event, and teardown runs at the next event boundary. The in-event continuation fence, which exists only to make a self-destroyed frame's own tail inert, is deleted.

This covers two callers: the frame's own handler, and another frame's handler reached through a nested cross-frame `dispatch-sync` inside the frame's event. The pending destroy claims the exact incarnation and takes the per-id reservation when it is requested.

Under the new contract:
- a self-destroying handler's `:db` and `:fx` land, including a dispatch to another, live frame, and its epoch settles `:ok`;
- every envelope still queued, in both lanes and including that event's own descendants, is dropped at the boundary and counted in one `:rf.frame/drain-interrupted`;
- `:on-destroy` runs once, after the event, and sees the committed db.

The normative text is written by the spec bead this one depends on ("Spec: destroy a frame at the next event boundary ..."). This is option C of the frame-lifetime decision, plus core-event-pipeline open question 1 answered "effects land, descendants dropped at the event boundary".

Decided 2026-10-16: Mike directed that every dossier recommendation an independent Codex review agreed with be actioned. Mike's instruction, verbatim:

> review all decisions. They now have a "Codex Recomendation" section.  Where the original recomendation matches the Code Recomendation, create a bead to action the decision.  And then remove the file.  Leave behind cases where the two differ materially.

Both reviews require the same order of work, so it is part of this ruling. First land the boundary teardown and prove the behaviours in the acceptance criteria. Only then delete the fences. The ruling names one condition that would reverse it: if a terminal record of the destroyed incarnation cannot be attributed to it without a fence, stop and report rather than moving the complexity elsewhere.

Decisive facts, read in source at trunk 89517e8f54:

1. **The fence is consulted at every hop.**
   - One `dispatch-sync` of a one-line handler resolves the frame registry 124 times. 105 of those resolutions come from `event-continuation-live?`, "consulted from TWENTY distinct sites in one pipeline run"; the comment calls the density "the design" (`implementation/core/src/re_frame/frame.cljc:1331-1362`).
   - The predicate is `frame.cljc:3815-3826`, carrying the `:allow-closing?` exemption for the private `:on-destroy` cascade.
   - Its router wrapper is `call-while-exact-owner` (`implementation/core/src/re_frame/router.cljc:70-91`, with a twin at `implementation/core/src/re_frame/fx.cljc:1370`).
   - `router.cljc` carries 54 `::stale-incarnation` sentinels.
2. **An existing JVM barrier test pins the behaviour this bead reverses.** `destroyed-incarnation-cannot-commit-its-returned-tail-into-replacement` (`implementation/core/test/re_frame/frame_destroy_incarnation_jvm_test.clj:177-256`) requires a self-destroying handler's returned `:db` and child dispatch to disappear. That file is 1,580 lines with 27 `deftest` forms.
3. **Stale async work has its own guards, and they stay.** These run at enqueue and at drain entry, not inside the event, and single threading does not remove stale completions:
   - captured async ops compare the exact incarnation at enqueue (`implementation/core/src/re_frame/capture_frame.cljc:81-124`);
   - `drain-try!` and `drain-block!` revalidate the incarnation inside the drain lock (`router.cljc:4079`, `:4135`).

Rejected options:
- **Keep the immediate cutoff (A).** A self-destroying handler's effects, including a dispatch to another frame, vanish without a word, and every future pipeline stage must remember its fence.
- **Thread confinement or a lifecycle scheduler (D).** It collides with `dispatch-sync`'s run-on-caller semantics. Add no executor, no per-frame thread and no general lifecycle scheduler.
- **Letting the destroying event's whole family finish.** That runs more handlers in a frame asked to die. Cleanup belongs in `:on-destroy`.
- **Keying the deferral on "the frame's own handler called it".** That misses a cross-frame nested `dispatch-sync`, which ClojureScript reaches too. The test is "this frame's drain is on the stack".

Supersedes: the immediate-cutoff behaviour pinned by `frame_destroy_incarnation_jvm_test.clj:177-256`. The spec bead supersedes the corresponding Spec 002 text. No EP or ruling bead is overturned.

**Not decided, and out of scope here.** Two questions stay open for Mike in the dossier `jvm-one-writer-per-frame.md`:
- generation publication (`set-generation!`, `frame.cljc:1696-1708`, core-frames A6);
- whether epoch's concurrency stress suite or its monitors shrink.

Touch neither.

## Work

**The pending destroy (`frame.cljc`, `router.cljc`)**
- **Entry.** At `destroy-frame!`'s entry, test whether the target incarnation's drain is on this stack. The router already stamps an `:in-drain?` marker that frame code reads (`frame.cljc:2201-2251`).
  - If it is, claim the exact incarnation, take the per-id reservation, record the pending destroy and return.
  - If it is not, tear down immediately, as today.
  - A second destroy of the same incarnation joins the pending request.
- **Boundary.** In the drain loop (`router.cljc:3959-4219`), when the dequeued event settles and a pending destroy exists for that incarnation, run teardown before any further dequeue:
  - drop both lanes and count them in one `:rf.frame/drain-interrupted`;
  - run `:on-destroy` once, after the event;
  - run the rest of the Spec 002 teardown order.
- **Reservation.** The pending destroy owns the reservation until its teardown has finished, including when the handler throws. The follow-on reservation bead moves the release point past the epoch notify. In this bead it must at least never be released before teardown begins.

**Delete the in-event fence, once the behaviours above are proven.** A grep for `stale-incarnation|continuation-live|exact-owner` at trunk finds these names in `router`, `fx`, `events`, `interceptor`, `frame`, `trace`, `trace/tooling`, `emit_substrate`, `error_emit`, `event_emit` and `late_bind/directory`. Census again before deleting, with a second token axis (`continue-before?`, `continue?`, `allow-closing?`). Delete:
- `call-while-exact-owner` (`router.cljc:70-91` and its twin `fx.cljc:1365-1380`);
- `::stale-incarnation` threading;
- `*exact-frame-owner-token*` (`fx.cljc`);
- `*continuation-predicate*` (`trace.cljc:68` and `trace/tooling.cljc`);
- `continue-before?` (`interceptor.cljc`);
- the `continue?` arity of the always-on fan-out (`emit_substrate.cljc:49-61`);
- the `:allow-closing?` exemption, and the in-event call sites of `event-continuation-live?`;
- the fence-density comment at `frame.cljc:1331-1362`, which is either rewritten or deleted.

**Deliver the always-on listeners after the event**, not synchronously inside the pipeline. Coordinate with `observability-listener-scheduler-and-fan-outs.md` (see Sequencing).

**Machines.** `implementation/machines/src/re_frame/machines/lifecycle_fx/destroy.cljc:70` onward is an "exact-incarnation destroy fence", and `finalize`, `timer` and `spawn` carry `owner-gone?` / `continue?` rechecks.
- Rechecks that only cut off an in-event self-destroy tail go with the core fence.
- Rechecks that guard stale async work stay.
- Keep one frame owner-token write helper (`swap-runtime-db-exact!`, `frame.cljc:2162`), because a machine node attempt is not a frame incarnation: an entry stamp in A must not authorise a write into B.

**Tests**
- Rewrite `frame_destroy_incarnation_jvm_test.clj` around boundary semantics. `:177-256` inverts: the returned `:db` and child dispatches land in A, and B never sees them. Its async and stale-destroy cases stay.
- Add tests for every acceptance case, on the JVM and, where the case reaches it, on ClojureScript.

**What deliberately stays:**
- the single-drainer CAS drain lock;
- the enqueue-time token compares (fact 3);
- the frame value as an exact-teardown token;
- restore's host-hook token;
- the per-id reservation and its fail-fast error;
- `make-frame`'s handler-scope refusal (`frame.cljc:3459-3472`), subject to the ordering choice in the acceptance criteria;
- the owner-only visibility of a first construction's provisional row. rf2-3x7nj.2.1 showed that hiding rows from the executor thread bites.

