The rf2-c0yds worker (#11483) found these comment sites, outside its fence, with the false premise that rf2-ktawq, rf2-phxak and rf2-c0yds removed elsewhere: a callback mid-reconcile destroys incarnation A and seats a same-id successor B.

That cannot happen inside A's own event drain:
- a destroy requested there stays pending until the drain ends;
- trace listeners run only after the drain;
- a frame destroy holds the id's reservation until step 10 has finished.

So the premise is true only where the code runs outside any drain: a direct call, epoch restore, or a tool write.

Sites in implementation/resources/src/re_frame/resources/ssr.cljc, at tip:
- ~1781 "incarnation A and seat a same-id successor B. A single check around the whole ...";
- ~1869 "successor B mid-reconcile could otherwise make it release B's live host ...";
- ~2072 ";; successor B mid-reconcile would otherwise make it release B's live ...";
- ~2304 "seat a same-id successor, so the incarnation is captured once and rechecked ...".

Also check the other resources hits of the same census and keep or restate each:
